{
  "requirement": "A node's structural hash MUST be the SHA-256 digest of an unambiguous encoding of\nits outline-relevant structure, including siblings its profile attaches to it:\nthe kinds of its named grammar nodes and, in source order, its text pieces. A\ntext piece MUST be the text of a leaf token, or text inside a node that no child\ntoken covers. Each text piece MUST be hashed as its ordered list of words, where\nwords are separated by whitespace, the whitespace itself, including leading and\ntrailing whitespace, is never hashed, and each word is encoded separately, so\n`ab cd` and `abcd` differ. The same rule MUST apply in every language. Comments\nMUST be excluded, except comment kinds the profile includes; the Rust profile MUST include doc comments. For an included comment, only its text and its\nprofile's comment group MUST be hashed, not its delimiter tokens such as `//`,\n`///`, `//!`, `/*`, `*/`, or a leading `*`; the Rust profile's groups are inner\nand outer doc comments. The words of consecutive included comments of one group\nMUST be hashed as one ordered list, so reflowing them across more or fewer lines\nor comments does not change the hash, while moving text between groups does. A `line`\nnode's hash MUST be computed by the same word rule over the line's text. A node's\nidentity hash MUST be computed the same way but MUST also exclude its outline\ndescendants and their attached siblings, so editing a descendant does not change\nits ancestors' selectors.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.structural_hash`, so I found no adequacy gap.\n\n- **SHA-256 of an unambiguous encoding, with kinds and nesting.**\n  - `hashes_include_grammar_kinds_nesting_and_sha256_digests` checks that identical text `x;` hashes differently under the Rust and Python grammars.\n  - The same test checks that the encoder gives different digests for nested and sibling kind sequences.\n  - It also pins exact SHA-256 vectors: the empty digest, and a tagged, length-prefixed text token for a `line` node.\n- **Text pieces hashed as words.**\n  - `prose_words_are_encoded_separately` checks `ab cd` against `abcd` in Rust doc comments and Markdown, and checks that whitespace and line breaks do not matter.\n  - `markdown_hashes_include_paragraph_text_between_punctuation_tokens` covers text inside a node that no child token covers.\n  - `plain_text_line_hashes_ignore_whitespace_but_not_word_breaks` covers leading, trailing and inner whitespace for `line` nodes. It also checks that the result equals `hash::line`.\n  - `string_literals_hash_as_words` covers leaf tokens in Rust, TOML and YAML block scalars.\n  - `reflowing_markdown_paragraphs_keeps_the_hash` covers Markdown paragraphs.\n- **Same rule in every language, with comments excluded.**\n  - `comments_are_excluded_from_hashes_in_every_other_profile` covers Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML. Each case checks that comment-only edits keep the hash and that a non-comment edit changes it.\n  - `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` covers the Rust plain-comment exclusion. It includes a plain comment placed between attached siblings, and checks that line moves leave the hash alone.\n- **Rust doc comments.**\n  - `reflowing_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash`, `editing_a_doc_comment_word_changes_the_hash` and `reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash` check that `///`, `/** * */`, `//!` and `/*! */` hash the same text identically. They also check that reflowing across lines or comments keeps the hash and that word edits or reordering change it.\n  - `inner_and_outer_doc_comments_hash_in_separate_groups` checks that moving text between the inner and outer groups changes the hash, while splitting words within one group does not.\n- **Attached siblings and identity hash.**\n  - `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants` covers Rust doc comments and attributes. A descendant's attached siblings change the ancestor's structural hash but not its identity hash. A node's own attached siblings do change its identity hash. A node without descendants has identical structural and identity hashes.\n  - `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing` covers the Python decorator profile.\n  - I checked the `attaches` functions in the profile sources: only the Rust and Python profiles attach siblings. The other profiles use `never`.\n- **Identity hashes in use.** `anonymous_selectors_use_identity_hashes`, `anonymous_identities_survive_reflow_and_sibling_edits_and_number_duplicates` and `outline_inputs_survive_moves_and_unhashed_comments_but_not_edits` check that anonymous selectors survive sibling insertion, reflow and edits to other nodes. They also check that selectors go stale when the node's own content changes.\n- **Redaction.** `hashes_do_not_depend_on_credential_values` is an extra check that hashes behave correctly with redaction.",
  "references": "43cc578b39e90b01d883677c61f178ab831120da3e5a9ccfbc4c9fed500d1f06",
